            <rel name="nonvolitional-result" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="sequence" type="multinuc"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="26" relname="span">The intuitiveness of a design increases the efficiency and ease of useof a product by preventing the user from having to manually complete acertain process, taking unecessary additional steps in completing aprocess, or repeating a process.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="31" relname="background">For example, a user wishes to play asong using an mp3 player on their computer.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="4" relname="circumstance">When the Open option on themusic player's interface is clicked,</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="34" relname="span">a choice is given of which folderto open the file from.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="35" relname="span">The user proceeds to open Folder A</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="elaboration">where thereare countless of stored files including word processing documents, HTMLfiles,  pictures, videos, and music.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="39" relname="span">An intuitive music player would"know" that the user is only looking for music files and display onlysuch files in the Open dialog box.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="37" relname="circumstance">The program would know that when auser wants to open a file,</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="37" relname="span">it can only look for mp3 files in the chosenfolder,</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="38" relname="span">not .txt, .ppt, mpeg, .wav, etc.,</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="10" relname="concession">even if all those filescoexisted in the same folder as the mp3 files.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="32" relname="elaboration">This intuitiveness isalso a form of error prevention that keeps the user from sorting outthrough incompatible file types, or risking damage to files by openingthem with wrong application.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="27" relname="span">Another example is when a particular application is given a command toopen an incompatible file type.</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="13" relname="elaboration">Or when there is not enough resourcesavailable to perform a process.</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="28" relname="antithesis">In intuitive program will know itslimitations and will not attempt to open the file regardless of thechances of freezing or shutting down the system.</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="28" relname="span">Instead, the intuitiveapplication would warn the user of its limitations with an errormessage, further directions, or even call on the operating system todisplay a list of available options,</segment>
        <segment id="17" parent="16" relname="elaboration">such as a display of other programsthat may (or may not) be able open that particular file type.</segment>
        <segment id="18" parent="25" relname="span">Still another example of intuitiveness relates to memorability</segment>
        <segment id="19" parent="18" relname="elaboration">when anapplication remembers your login, password, or demographic informationto prevent redundancy;</segment>
        <segment id="20" parent="25" relname="elaboration">also, when the AOL client application alerts theuser that they've got mail before having a chance to go to the mailbox.</segment>
        <segment id="21" parent="24" relname="nonvolitional-result">Such transparent processes provide subtle instances of  intuitiveness.</segment>
